    "story": "The man on the ground does not want to move, and the man standing is not going to let that stop him.\n\nHe bends over the fallen soldier and takes both his arms, hauling him up out of the grass a few inches at a time. The fallen man's rifle is still on his back, and the grass is in his collar, and the standing man's knees are shaking with the effort. Nobody else is coming.\n\nHe gets the man to his knees. From there, the man can get the rest of the way himself.",
    "notes": "Two men and one lift: that is the image. A soldier bent double over a fallen comrade, hauling him up by the arms while the fallen man reaches up to meet him, the grass and the vertical frame keeping it intimate and close. The image is not about the battle at all — it is about the moment after, when one man decides the other is not staying down. Collectors who want a WWII image that is about comradeship rather than combat will find this one does the lifting for them.",
