    "story": "They move in a loose line across the grass, five men walking away from where they started, and none of them is looking back. The packs ride high on their shoulders and the rifles hang where they can be reached, and the field ahead is wide enough that their boots barely make a sound in it.\n\nThe grass is wet from the night and it clings to their boots. They have been carrying the packs long enough that the weight has settled into the rhythm of the walk. The talk has gone quiet. There is nothing to talk about yet. The field is still just a field.\n\nAt the far edge of the field is a hedgerow, and past the hedgerow is whatever the hedgerow is hiding. They are walking toward it at the pace the packs allow, and nobody is in a hurry to find out what is on the other side.",
    "notes": "The view from behind is what makes this one: five men in a loose line, packs high on their shoulders, rifles slung, moving across wet grass under a pale sky. The black and white treatment lets the packs and the rifles carry the detail, and the squad's direction — away, toward the hedgerow at the far edge — gives the frame its quiet momentum. It pairs naturally with the running frames from the same field, and it stands on its own as a portrait of a squad in motion. For a collector who wants the advance without the heroics.",
