    "story": "The grass is wet against his knees and the rifle is heavy at his shoulder. Holding still takes more out of a man than running does, and he has been holding still for a long time now, with the sights lined up on the treeline at the end of the field.\n\nThe men on either side of him are doing the same — knees in the grass, rifles up, the field in front of them and nothing moving in it. The quiet sits on them all the same way. Somewhere behind them, boots are still coming up the slope.\n\nHe is not thinking about the shot. He is thinking about the weight of the rifle, and the cold working into his knees, and the fact that there is nothing out there to aim at yet — and hoping it stays that way.",
    "notes": "The stillness is what you are buying here: a whole firing line kneeling in wet grass, rifles up, nothing fired yet. The foreground soldier's aim, the staggered line behind him, and the tall grass closing around their knees give the frame a patience most battle photographs skip — the moment before the noise, held. The black and white treatment keeps the attention on the line itself, and the low angle puts the viewer down in the grass with them. For a collector who wants the held breath rather than the blast.",
