    "story": "He has his hands on the man's chest and he is not pressing down hard — just enough to feel the ribs move, to know the man is still in there. The blood is on his sleeves already, and the dirt is under his nails, and neither one matters.\n\nThe man on the ground is awake, and that is the first thing the medic checked. He is hurting, and the medic can see it in his face, and he is talking to him — low, steady, the same few words over and over, because a voice keeps a man here when the pain is pulling him somewhere else.\n\nBehind them another man is kneeling and watching, and further back the rest of them are lying flat in the grass. The medic does not look up. The man under his hands is still here, and that is as far as the morning has gotten.",
    "notes": "Look at the hands first: kneeling in the grass, on the man's chest, leaning in close while the rest of the squad lies flat around them. There is no action here, and that is the point — the work is in the hands, and the black and white treatment keeps the hands and the faces at the front of the frame. The long grass closing around the men makes the scene feel private, almost quiet, and that quiet is what separates this frame from the rest of the advance. For a collector who wants the war's human center rather than its noise, this is where to look.",
