    "story": "The straps have worked their way down to the bone of his shoulders, and the pack has been on his back long enough for that to stop mattering. The rifle hangs off his shoulder by its sling and swings against his thigh with every step, and he does not bother to still it.\n\nHis mouth is open — not shouting, just breathing, the kind of breathing that comes when the slope is steeper than it looked from the bottom. He is looking to the right, toward the man behind him, and he is not stopping.\n\nThe ground ahead is broken and pale, and the sky is doing nothing for anybody. He walks because the pack is already on and the rifle is already slung, and the sooner he gets where he is going, the sooner both come off.",
    "notes": "Everything he owns is on his back: a heavy pack, a slung rifle, straps cutting into the shoulders, and a mouth open with the effort of the slope. The second soldier behind him gives the frame its depth, and the pale broken ground gives it its emptiness — and in the middle of it, a man doing the hard part of the war, which is carrying everything he owns across open ground. The black and white treatment keeps the kit and the exertion at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the soldier's burden rather than the soldier's pose, this is the frame.",
