    "story": "His hand is up and his mouth is open, and he is holding the shape of it while the men behind him work out what he is telling them. The dirt is in his cuffs and the netting on his helmet is coming loose at the edge, and he does not care about either one.\n\nHe is young — younger than the men behind him, and they know it, and he knows they know it. That is why the hand is up so straight, and why the voice is coming out of him a little louder than it needs to be. He is not asking them. He is telling them.\n\nWhat he is telling them is the ground in front of him: which part of it is open, and which part of it is not, and where the next man should put his feet. The hand stays up until they move. It has not come down yet.",
    "notes": "Everything in this frame hangs on the hand: a young soldier's hand straight up, his mouth open, the dirt on his cuffs and the netting coming loose on his helmet. It is a portrait of command at its youngest — a man telling the men behind him where to put their feet, and doing it with a raised hand rather than a shout. The low bank, the pale sky, and the black and white treatment keep the attention on the signal itself, and that is what makes this frame rare: most signal photographs are about the men moving; this one is about the man doing the signaling. For a collector who wants the gesture rather than the action, this one earns its wall.",
