    "story": "The white bag is what anybody sees first, and he knows it. It hangs across his chest, bright against the smock and the haze, and there is nothing he can do about it — the bag holds what he needs, and what he needs does not come in a darker color.\n\nThe rifle is across his body and his hand is on it, and the two men behind him are little more than shapes in the haze. The haze is thin enough to walk through and thick enough to hide in, and he is doing both at once.\n\nThe bag is heavy and the rifle is heavy, and the smock is damp at the shoulders. He walks because the haze does not last, and when it lifts, he wants to be somewhere else.",
    "notes": "Start with the white bag: a bright shape against a smock and a haze, the shape in the frame that will not blend in. The soldier's grip on his rifle, the two men half-lost behind him, and the haze closing around them all give the image a quiet tension — a patrol that is hidden and exposed at the same time. The black and white treatment lets the bag, the smock, and the haze carry the frame, and the low, forward-leaning pose gives it a momentum most patrol frames miss. For a collector who wants the odd detail — the shape that does not belong — this frame delivers it.",
