    "story": "He is down on one knee and the rifle is planted in the grass beside him, the pack still on his back and the ground soft under his knee. He lets the knee sink. The weight of the pack is a thing he has stopped arguing with.\n\nHe is looking off to the left, and the trees behind him are still. Nothing is moving in the field in front of him, and he is watching for the moment that changes.\n\nThe knee will not stay down forever. The pack will come off his back when they get where they are going, and the rifle will come up off the grass, and he will be on his feet again — but not yet. Not until he knows what he is looking at.",
    "notes": "A pause, held on one knee: a soldier down in the grass, the rifle planted upright beside him, the pack still on his back and the field empty in front of him. There is no action here, and that is what makes it rare — most of the war set is running or aiming or carrying, and this frame is the pause between them, the breath the rest of the set does not take. The black and white treatment keeps the kneel, the rifle, and the quiet field at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the still point in a set full of movement, this is the frame to hang.",
