    "story": "The steel was built to stop tanks, and it is doing its job on the tanks. It was not built for a man, and a man is climbing it anyway, one boot on a beam and the other feeling for the next one, the rifle on his back catching on the steel with every move.\n\nThe smoke is thick on the other side, and he cannot see what is past the obstacle. He can hear it, though — boots, and metal on metal, and another man doing what he is doing, a little further on.\n\nHe gets his second boot up and over, and the steel is behind him. The smoke is in front of him, and the ground past the smoke is the part he has not seen yet. He goes anyway.",
    "notes": "The obstacle makes the frame. A crossed-steel hedgehog built to stop tanks, and a single man climbing over it with a rifle on his back — the scale of the steel against the scale of the man is what this photograph is about, and the smoke behind the obstacle keeps the far side of the story out of reach. The mid-climb balance, the pack, and the steel beams give the image a shape most battle photographs do not have, and the black and white treatment keeps the steel and the smoke at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the obstacle crossed by one man on foot, the proof is here.",
