    "story": "The water is cold enough to make them curse under their breath, and it is up to their ankles and climbing. The bottom is mud, and it shifts under their boots with every step, and the rifles are held high so the water does not get into the actions.\n\nThe lead man is watching the far bank, and the man behind him is watching the lead man's back. The far bank is grass and a fence, and past the fence is a line of trees. None of it is moving. That is the part that keeps them crouched.\n\nThe water is past their ankles now, and the mud is thicker than it was at the near bank. They keep going because the far bank is dry, and dry is worth the cold.",
    "notes": "The water is what makes this one different from the rest of the advance frames — two men up to their ankles in a cold stream, rifles held high so the actions stay dry, the far bank still to reach. The low crouch, the mud, and the way the water catches the light around their boots give the frame a texture the dry-ground photographs never get, and the black and white treatment keeps the men and the water at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the advance at its wettest — the ford, not the road — this is the ford to own.",
