    "description": "Two men in civilian clothes take cover behind wooden barrels in this painterly World War II battle photograph by Wayne Heim, part of the Facing History collection. One man kneels behind a barrel with his rifle resting on its rim, aiming out across the grass, while the second man watches from behind the next barrel, a rough stone wall at their backs. The oak barrels, the stone wall, and the men's flat caps give the image its character: an improvised firing position, held by men in their own clothes.\n\nPart of a broader body of work, this piece is one of a series of images, crafted to honor the enduring legacy of the Facing History collection and the historical moments it represents. It is suited for collection within this narrative series, bringing depth, historical perspective, and emotion to any fine art collection or art lover's space.\n\nTwo men, two barrels, and a wall at their backs. © Wayne Heim",
