    "story": "The barrel is oak and it smells of it, even now, and the rifle is resting on the rim where the wood is worn smooth by hands. He is kneeling behind it with his cheek to the stock, and the man beside him is watching the same ground, and both of them are in their own clothes.\n\nThe stone wall behind them is the back of a barn, and the barn is empty. The ground in front of them has not moved all morning, and neither have they.\n\nHe is aiming at something out in front of him, and the man beside him is watching the same stretch of ground. The shot has not come yet. The barrel is solid under his hands, and the wall is solid at his back, and solid is what he is counting on.",
    "notes": "No uniforms, no sandbags — just oak barrels, a stone wall, and two men in their own clothes, which is what makes this frame unlike anything else in the set. The rifleman's cheek to the stock, the second man's watch, and the wall at their backs give the image a civilian gravity the uniformed frames never carry, and the black and white treatment lets the wood, the stone, and the flat caps hold the frame. For a collector who wants the war as it was fought by the people who lived where it happened, this one belongs on the wall.",
