    "story": "The fist is up and the men behind him can see it, and that is the message. It is not a wave and it is not a cheer — it is a hand made into a shape, and the shape means stop, and the men behind him have stopped.\n\nThe weapon is in his other hand, and it is pointing at the sky because there is nothing in front of him to point it at yet. The stone wall is at his back and the smoke is in front of him.\n\nThe smoke moves across the ground in front of him, and the men behind him settle into their places, and the fist does not move.",
    "notes": "A raised fist is rarer than a raised rifle in this set, and this is the frame that has one — a soldier mid-shout, fist high, submachine gun in his other hand, a grenade at his chest and a stone wall at his back. The clenched hand, the open mouth, and the weapon pointed at the sky give the image a volume the quieter frames never reach, and the black and white treatment keeps the fist and the face at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the war loud — the signal given at full voice, not whispered — this one turns the volume up.",
