    "story": "One barrel is not enough for two men, and they know it. The first man is down behind it with the rifle out, and the second man is leaning over his back, and half of the second man is out in the open, and that is the way it is.\n\nHis weight is on the first man's shoulders, and the first man is taking it without a word. There is a pipe behind them and a barrel in front of them, and that is all the cover there is.\n\nThe rifle is out and the first man has it shouldered, and the second man's hands are on the first man's back. They are holding still, and the barrel is smaller than both of them, and neither of them says anything about it.",
    "notes": "One barrel, two men — that is the photograph, and it is also the tension. The lead soldier aims out across the grass from behind the barrel while the second man leans over his back, half out in the open, and the pipe and the tree behind them do nothing to help. The shared cover, the lean, and the grass on every side give the image a shape the single-man frames never have, and the black and white treatment keeps the barrel and the two helmets at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the pair — two men doing the work of one piece of cover — this one is the pair to own.",
