    "story": "The rifle is long, and the length of it is the point. It reaches past the barrels and out over the grass, and the man behind it is lined up with it, so the rifle and the man make one straight line out of the crouch.\n\nThe second man is crouched behind him, and he is not looking where the rifle is looking. He is looking at the rifle, and at the first man's hands, because the hands are the part that tells him when the shot is coming.\n\nThe grass in front of the barrels is empty, and the rifle is pointed at the emptiness. The rifle has not gone off yet. The man behind it has not moved. The second man has not moved either.",
    "notes": "Hold a rifle level long enough and it becomes a line — and this frame is built on one: a bolt-action held horizontal at shoulder height, its muzzle past the barrels, the man behind it lined up with it and the second man crouched behind him. The horizontal line of the rifle against the vertical lines of the barrels gives the image a geometry the other barrel frames never get, and the black and white treatment keeps the rifle and the two helmets at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the aim rather than the shot — the held line, not the firing — this one keeps it level.",
