    "story": "He cannot see. The bandage is over his eyes and the world has gone dark for him, and the two men on either side of him are his eyes now.\n\nThe man on the right has a red cross on his arm, and the man on the left has a rifle on his back, and the two of them are walking him forward between them, one hand on each of his arms, and the ground under his boots is the ground they are choosing for him.\n\nHe is not fighting them. He is letting them walk him, and his feet are finding the ground slow and careful, trusting the hands on his arms. The dark is not going anywhere. The walking is what there is.",
    "notes": "The bandage is what you see first, and it is what you remember — a white cloth over the wounded man's eyes, and two comrades on either side of him, walking him forward between them. The medic's red-cross armband, the rifle on the other man's back, and the bowed heads give the image a tenderness the battle frames rarely carry, and the black and white treatment lets the bandage, the hands, and the men's uniforms hold the frame. For a collector who wants the war's human cost — a man who cannot see, led by the men who can — there is nothing else in the set like it.",
