    "story": "What is he photographing? The camera is up and the camera is pointed up — at the sky, at the tree, at the building, at something above him, and the something above him is the picture, and the picture is the one he is taking, and the taking is the camera, and the camera is his.\n\nThe goggles are on his helmet, pushed up on the helmet, and the pushed-up is the not-using — the goggles are not the eyes, and the camera is the eyes, and the eyes are the looking, and the looking is the camera.\n\nThere is another man's shoulder in the foreground, and the shoulder is out of focus, and the out of focus is the other man, and the other man is the one the camera is not looking at, and the not-looking-at is the foreground, and the foreground is the shoulder.",
    "notes": "A soldier with a camera is a frame the war set saves for the end — a German soldier in a steel helmet with goggles pushed up on it, holding a small camera to his face, pointing it upward, a tree trunk behind him and another soldier's shoulder out of focus in the foreground. The camera, the goggles, and the upward look give the image a shape the action frames never have, and the black and white treatment keeps the camera and the man at the front of the frame. For a collector who wants the photographer — the camera, the goggles, and the lens pointed up — this one holds it.",
